# Flow — RTF Async Callout Pattern

## Callouts are not allowed on synchronous Flow paths

A Record-Triggered Flow cannot make HTTP callouts on its synchronous `RecordAfterSave` path. Attempting to call an Apex action with `callout=true` from a sync path throws a runtime error.

## Use an AsyncAfterCommit scheduled path

Route callout work through a scheduled path with `pathType: AsyncAfterCommit`:

```xml
<start>
    <filterLogic>and</filterLogic>
    <filters>
        <field>StageName</field>
        <operator>EqualTo</operator>
        <value><stringValue>Commit</stringValue></value>
    </filters>
    <filters>
        <field>StageName</field>
        <operator>IsChanged</operator>
        <value><booleanValue>true</booleanValue></value>
    </filters>
    <object>Opportunity</object>
    <recordTriggerType>Update</recordTriggerType>
    <scheduledPaths>
        <connector>
            <targetReference>Run_Orchestrator</targetReference>
        </connector>
        <pathType>AsyncAfterCommit</pathType>
    </scheduledPaths>
    <triggerType>RecordAfterSave</triggerType>
</start>
```

## Delegate all logic to a single Apex invocable action

Do NOT chain multiple Flow elements (decisions, record updates, screen components) after the async path. Async-path Flow record updates have their own governor limits and DML restrictions that differ from Apex.

Delegate everything — callout, risk computation, record update, task creation — to one `@InvocableMethod(callout=true)` Apex class:

```xml
<actionCalls>
    <name>Run_Orchestrator</name>
    <actionName>MyOrchestratorClass</actionName>
    <actionType>apex</actionType>
    <inputParameters>
        <name>recordId</name>
        <value><elementReference>$Record.Id</elementReference></value>
    </inputParameters>
    <storeOutputAutomatically>false</storeOutputAutomatically>
</actionCalls>
```

## Key rules

- `processType` must be `AutoLaunchedFlow` for RTFs
- The async scheduled path fires after the transaction commits — `$Record` contains the saved values
- Pass only the record Id to Apex; fetch all other data inside Apex via SOQL
- All DML on the triggering record must happen in Apex — a single consolidated `update` at the end
